        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Galvanometer &#2348;&#2367;&#2332;&#2354;&#2368; &#2325;&#2368; &#2358;&#2325;&#2381;&#2340;&#2367; &#2344;&#2366;&#2346;&#2344;&#2375; &#2325;&#2366; &#2351;&#2306;&#2340;&#2381;&#2352;",
          "html": "<span class=\"eqt\">Galvanometer</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2348;&#2367;&#2332;&#2354;&#2368; &#2325;&#2368; &#2358;&#2325;&#2381;&#2340;&#2367; &#2344;&#2366;&#2346;&#2344;&#2375; &#2325;&#2366; &#2351;&#2306;&#2340;&#2381;&#2352;</span>"
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Psophometer &#2360;&#2379;&#2347;&#2364;&#2379;&#2350;&#2368;&#2335;&#2352;",
          "html": "<span class=\"eqt\">Psophometer</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2360;&#2379;&#2347;&#2364;&#2379;&#2350;&#2368;&#2335;&#2352;</span>"
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Potentiometer &#2340;&#2344;&#2366;&#2357; &#2344;&#2366;&#2346;&#2344;&#2375; &#2325;&#2366; &#2351;&#2306;&#2340;&#2381;&#2352;",
          "html": "<span class=\"eqt\">Potentiometer</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2340;&#2344;&#2366;&#2357; &#2344;&#2366;&#2346;&#2344;&#2375; &#2325;&#2366; &#2351;&#2306;&#2340;&#2381;&#2352;</span>"
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Ammeter &#2319;&#2350;&#2381;&#2350;&#2367;&#2335;&#2352;",
          "html": "<span class=\"eqt\">Ammeter</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2319;&#2350;&#2381;&#2350;&#2367;&#2335;&#2352;</span>"
        }
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "A",
      "solution": "<p>&#183; A <b>galvanometer</b> is an <b>electromechanical instrument</b> used to <b>detect and measure small electric currents</b>.</p><p>&#183; It works on the principle that a <b>magnetic field exerts a force on a current-carrying conductor</b>, causing <b>needle deflection</b> proportional to the current.</p><p>&#183; It's primarily used in <b>laboratories</b> and as the basis for constructing other devices like <b>ammeters</b> and <b>voltmeters</b>.</p><p><b>Other Options:</b></p><p>&#183; <b>Psophometer</b>: Measures noise in telephone circuits (not current).</p><p>&#183; <b>Potentiometer</b>: Measures voltage (potential difference) precisely.</p><p>&#183; <b>Ammeter</b>: Measures current <b>directly</b>, often based on galvanometer design, but can handle higher currents.</p>",
      "images": [],
      "subject": null,
      "topic": null,
      "difficulty": null
    },
    {
      "id": "44c5b6fbfbe8-q6",
      "number": 6,
      "question": "In the Right-Hand Thumb Rule, the thumb is directed towards the direction of: &#2342;&#2366;&#2361;&#2367;&#2344;&#2375; &#2361;&#2366;&#2341; &#2325;&#2375; &#2309;&#2306;&#2327;&#2370;&#2336;&#2375; &#2325;&#2375; &#2344;&#2367;&#2351;&#2350; &#2350;&#2375;&#2306;, &#2309;&#2306;&#2327;&#2370;&#2336;&#2375; &#2325;&#2379; &#2311;&#2360; &#2342;&#2367;&#2358;&#2366; &#2325;&#2368; &#2323;&#2352; &#2344;&#2367;&#2352;&#2381;&#2342;&#2375;&#2358;&#2367;&#2340; &#2325;&#2367;&#2351;&#2366; &#2332;&#2366;&#2340;&#2366; &#2361;&#2376;:",
      "questionHtml": "<span class=\"eqt\"><p>In the Right-Hand Thumb Rule, the thumb is directed towards the direction of:</p></span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2342;&#2366;&#2361;&#2367;&#2344;&#2375; &#2361;&#2366;&#2341; &#2325;&#2375; &#2309;&#2306;&#2327;&#2370;&#2336;&#2375; &#2325;&#2375; &#2344;&#2367;&#2351;&#2350; &#2350;&#2375;&#2306;, &#2309;&#2306;&#2327;&#2370;&#2336;&#2375; &#2325;&#2379; &#2311;&#2360; &#2342;&#2367;&#2358;&#2366; &#2325;&#2368; &#2323;&#2352; &#2344;&#2367;&#2352;&#2381;&#2342;&#2375;&#2358;&#2367;&#2340; &#2325;&#2367;&#2351;&#2366; &#2332;&#2366;&#2340;&#2366; &#2361;&#2376;:</span>",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Current &#2350;&#2380;&#2332;&#2370;&#2342;&#2366;",
          "html": "<span class=\"eqt\">Current</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2350;&#2380;&#2332;&#2370;&#2342;&#2366;</span>"
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Electric field &#2357;&#2367;&#2342;&#2381;&#2351;&#2369;&#2340; &#2325;&#2381;&#2359;&#2375;&#2340;&#2381;&#2352;",
          "html": "<span class=\"eqt\">Electric field</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2357;&#2367;&#2342;&#2381;&#2351;&#2369;&#2340; &#2325;&#2381;&#2359;&#2375;&#2340;&#2381;&#2352;</span>"
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Motion of the conductor &#2325;&#2306;&#2337;&#2325;&#2381;&#2335;&#2352; &#2325;&#2368; &#2327;&#2340;&#2367;",
          "html": "<span class=\"eqt\">Motion of the conductor</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2325;&#2306;&#2337;&#2325;&#2381;&#2335;&#2352; &#2325;&#2368; &#2327;&#2340;&#2367;</span>"
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Magnetic field &#2330;&#2369;&#2306;&#2348;&#2325;&#2368;&#2351; &#2325;&#2381;&#2359;&#2375;&#2340;&#2381;&#2352;",
          "html": "<span class=\"eqt\">Magnetic field</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2330;&#2369;&#2306;&#2348;&#2325;&#2368;&#2351; &#2325;&#2381;&#2359;&#2375;&#2340;&#2381;&#2352;</span>"
        }
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "A",
      "solution": "<p>&bull; The Right-Hand Thumb Rule, formulated by John Ambrose Fleming, is used in electromagnetism to determine the direction of the magnetic field around a current-carrying conductor.</p><p>&bull; According to this rule:</p><p>o If the right-hand thumb points in the direction of the current,</p><p>o The curled fingers indicate the direction of the magnetic field.</p><p>&bull; This rule is essential in understanding electromagnetism and its applications, such as in electric motors and generators.</p>",
      "images": [],
      "subject": null,
      "topic": null,
      "difficulty": null
    },
    {
      "id": "44c5b6fbfbe8-q7",
      "number": 7,
      "question": "What do you call the effect of splitting of a spectral line into several components in the presence of a static magnetic field? &#2319;&#2325; &#2360;&#2381;&#2341;&#2367;&#2352; &#2330;&#2369;&#2306;&#2348;&#2325;&#2368;&#2351; &#2325;&#2381;&#2359;&#2375;&#2340;&#2381;&#2352; &#2325;&#2368; &#2313;&#2346;&#2360;&#2381;&#2341;&#2367;&#2340;&#2367; &#2350;&#2375;&#2306; &#2360;&#2381;&#2346;&#2375;&#2325;&#2381;&#2335;&#2381;&#2352;&#2350;&#2368; &#2352;&#2375;&#2326;&#2366; &#2325;&#2375; &#2325;&#2312; &#2360;&#2306;&#2328;&#2335;&#2325;&#2379;&#2306; &#2350;&#2375;&#2306; &#2357;&#2367;&#2346;&#2366;&#2335;&#2344; &#2361;&#2379;&#2344;&#2375; &#2325;&#2375; &#2346;&#2381;&#2352;&#2349;&#2366;&#2357; &#2325;&#2379; &#2325;&#2381;&#2351;&#2366; &#2325;&#2361;&#2366; &#2332;&#2366;&#2340;&#2366; &#2361;&#2376;?",
      "questionHtml": "<span class=\"eqt\"><p>What do you call the effect of splitting of a spectral line into several components in the presence of a static magnetic field?</p></span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\"><p>&#2319;&#2325; &#2360;&#2381;&#2341;&#2367;&#2352; &#2330;&#2369;&#2306;&#2348;&#2325;&#2368;&#2351; &#2325;&#2381;&#2359;&#2375;&#2340;&#2381;&#2352; &#2325;&#2368; &#2313;&#2346;&#2360;&#2381;&#2341;&#2367;&#2340;&#2367; &#2350;&#2375;&#2306; &#2360;&#2381;&#2346;&#2375;&#2325;&#2381;&#2335;&#2381;&#2352;&#2350;&#2368; &#2352;&#2375;&#2326;&#2366; &#2325;&#2375; &#2325;&#2312; &#2360;&#2306;&#2328;&#2335;&#2325;&#2379;&#2306; &#2350;&#2375;&#2306; &#2357;&#2367;&#2346;&#2366;&#2335;&#2344; &#2361;&#2379;&#2344;&#2375; &#2325;&#2375; &#2346;&#2381;&#2352;&#2349;&#2366;&#2357; &#2325;&#2379; &#2325;&#2381;&#2351;&#2366; &#2325;&#2361;&#2366; &#2332;&#2366;&#2340;&#2366; &#2361;&#2376;?</p></span>",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Askaryan effect &#2319;&#2360;&#2381;&#2325;&#2376;&#2352;&#2367;&#2351;&#2344; &#2346;&#2381;&#2352;&#2349;&#2366;&#2357;",
          "html": "<span class=\"eqt\">Askaryan effect</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2319;&#2360;&#2381;&#2325;&#2376;&#2352;&#2367;&#2351;&#2344; &#2346;&#2381;&#2352;&#2349;&#2366;&#2357;</span>"
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Zeeman effect &#2332;&#2368;&#2350;&#2344; &#2346;&#2381;&#2352;&#2349;&#2366;&#2357;",
          "html": "<span class=\"eqt\">Zeeman effect</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2332;&#2368;&#2350;&#2344; &#2346;&#2381;&#2352;&#2349;&#2366;&#2357;</span>"
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Bezold effect &#2348;&#2375;&#2332;&#2377;&#2354;&#2381;&#2337; &#2346;&#2381;&#2352;&#2349;&#2366;&#2357;",
          "html": "<span class=\"eqt\">Bezold effect</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2348;&#2375;&#2332;&#2377;&#2354;&#2381;&#2337; &#2346;&#2381;&#2352;&#2349;&#2366;&#2357;</span>"
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Domino effect &#2337;&#2377;&#2350;&#2367;&#2344;&#2379; &#2346;&#2381;&#2352;&#2349;&#2366;&#2357;",
          "html": "<span class=\"eqt\">Domino effect</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2337;&#2377;&#2350;&#2367;&#2344;&#2379; &#2346;&#2381;&#2352;&#2349;&#2366;&#2357;</span>"
        }
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "B",
      "solution": "<p>The effect of splitting of a spectral line into several components in the presence of a static magnetic field is called the <b>Zeeman effect</b>. It is named after the Dutch physicist Pieter Zeeman, who discovered it in 1896 and won a Nobel prize for this discovery.</p>",
      "images": [],
      "subject": null,
      "topic": null,
      "difficulty": null
    },
    {
      "id": "44c5b6fbfbe8-q8",
      "number": 8,
      "question": "Which of the following elements is highly effective for making a permanent magnet? &#2344;&#2367;&#2350;&#2381;&#2344;&#2354;&#2367;&#2326;&#2367;&#2340; &#2350;&#2375;&#2306; &#2360;&#2375; &#2325;&#2380;&#2344;-&#2360;&#2366; &#2340;&#2340;&#2381;&#2357; &#2360;&#2381;&#2341;&#2366;&#2351;&#2368; &#2330;&#2369;&#2306;&#2348;&#2325; &#2348;&#2344;&#2366;&#2344;&#2375; &#2325;&#2375; &#2354;&#2367;&#2319; &#2309;&#2340;&#2381;&#2351;&#2343;&#2367;&#2325; &#2346;&#2381;&#2352;&#2349;&#2366;&#2357;&#2368; &#2361;&#2376;?",
      "questionHtml": "<span class=\"eqt\"><p>Which of the following elements is highly effective for making a permanent magnet?</p></span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\"><p>&#2344;&#2367;&#2350;&#2381;&#2344;&#2354;&#2367;&#2326;&#2367;&#2340; &#2350;&#2375;&#2306; &#2360;&#2375; &#2325;&#2380;&#2344;-&#2360;&#2366; &#2340;&#2340;&#2381;&#2357; &#2360;&#2381;&#2341;&#2366;&#2351;&#2368; &#2330;&#2369;&#2306;&#2348;&#2325; &#2348;&#2344;&#2366;&#2344;&#2375; &#2325;&#2375; &#2354;&#2367;&#2319; &#2309;&#2340;&#2381;&#2351;&#2343;&#2367;&#2325; &#2346;&#2381;&#2352;&#2349;&#2366;&#2357;&#2368; &#2361;&#2376;?</p></span>",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Zinc &#2332;&#2367;&#2306;&#2325;",
          "html": "<span class=\"eqt\">Zinc</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2332;&#2367;&#2306;&#2325;</span>"
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Aluminium &#2309;&#2354;&#2381;&#2351;&#2369;&#2350;&#2368;&#2344;&#2367;&#2351;&#2350;",
          "html": "<span class=\"eqt\">Aluminium</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2309;&#2354;&#2381;&#2351;&#2369;&#2350;&#2368;&#2344;&#2367;&#2351;&#2350;</span>"
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Copper &#2325;&#2377;&#2346;&#2352;",
          "html": "<span class=\"eqt\">Copper</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2325;&#2377;&#2346;&#2352;</span>"
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Steel &#2360;&#2381;&#2335;&#2368;&#2354;",
          "html": "<span class=\"eqt\">Steel</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2360;&#2381;&#2335;&#2368;&#2354;</span>"
        }
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "D",
      "solution": "<p><b>Steel</b> is highly effective for making permanent magnets due to its ability to retain magnetization after being magnetized. This property arises from the alignment of its atomic structure, making it a suitable material for creating strong and long-lasting magnets.</p>",
      "images": [],
      "subject": null,
      "topic": null,
      "difficulty": null
    },
    {
      "id": "44c5b6fbfbe8-q9",
      "number": 9,
      "question": "Which famous experiment was conducted by Michael Faraday in 1831? &#2350;&#2366;&#2311;&#2325;&#2354; &#2347;&#2376;&#2352;&#2366;&#2337;&#2375; &#2344;&#2375; 1831 &#2350;&#2375;&#2306; &#2325;&#2380;&#2344; &#2360;&#2366; &#2346;&#2381;&#2352;&#2360;&#2367;&#2342;&#2381;&#2343; &#2346;&#2381;&#2352;&#2351;&#2379;&#2327; &#2325;&#2367;&#2351;&#2366; &#2341;&#2366;?",
      "questionHtml": "<span class=\"eqt\"><p>Which famous experiment was conducted by Michael Faraday in 1831?</p></span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2350;&#2366;&#2311;&#2325;&#2354; &#2347;&#2376;&#2352;&#2366;&#2337;&#2375; &#2344;&#2375; 1831 &#2350;&#2375;&#2306; &#2325;&#2380;&#2344; &#2360;&#2366; &#2346;&#2381;&#2352;&#2360;&#2367;&#2342;&#2381;&#2343; &#2346;&#2381;&#2352;&#2351;&#2379;&#2327; &#2325;&#2367;&#2351;&#2366; &#2341;&#2366;?</span>",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Discovery of quantum magnetometers &#2325;&#2381;&#2357;&#2366;&#2306;&#2335;&#2350; &#2350;&#2376;&#2327;&#2381;&#2344;&#2375;&#2335;&#2379;&#2350;&#2368;&#2335;&#2352; &#2325;&#2368; &#2326;&#2379;&#2332;",
          "html": "<span class=\"eqt\">Discovery of quantum magnetometers</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2325;&#2381;&#2357;&#2366;&#2306;&#2335;&#2350; &#2350;&#2376;&#2327;&#2381;&#2344;&#2375;&#2335;&#2379;&#2350;&#2368;&#2335;&#2352; &#2325;&#2368; &#2326;&#2379;&#2332;</span>"
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Discovery of the law of elasticity &#2354;&#2379;&#2330; &#2325;&#2375; &#2344;&#2367;&#2351;&#2350; &#2325;&#2368; &#2326;&#2379;&#2332;",
          "html": "<span class=\"eqt\">Discovery of the law of elasticity</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2354;&#2379;&#2330; &#2325;&#2375; &#2344;&#2367;&#2351;&#2350; &#2325;&#2368; &#2326;&#2379;&#2332;</span>"
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Discovery of electromagnetic induction &#2357;&#2367;&#2342;&#2381;&#2351;&#2369;&#2340; &#2330;&#2369;&#2350;&#2381;&#2348;&#2325;&#2368;&#2351; &#2346;&#2381;&#2352;&#2375;&#2352;&#2339; &#2325;&#2368; &#2326;&#2379;&#2332;",
          "html": "<span class=\"eqt\">Discovery of electromagnetic induction</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2357;&#2367;&#2342;&#2381;&#2351;&#2369;&#2340; &#2330;&#2369;&#2350;&#2381;&#2348;&#2325;&#2368;&#2351; &#2346;&#2381;&#2352;&#2375;&#2352;&#2339; &#2325;&#2368; &#2326;&#2379;&#2332;</span>"
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Discovery of natural radioactivity &#2346;&#2381;&#2352;&#2366;&#2325;&#2371;&#2340;&#2367;&#2325; &#2352;&#2375;&#2337;&#2367;&#2351;&#2379;&#2343;&#2352;&#2381;&#2350;&#2367;&#2340;&#2366; &#2325;&#2368; &#2326;&#2379;&#2332;",
          "html": "<span class=\"eqt\">Discovery of natural radioactivity</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2346;&#2381;&#2352;&#2366;&#2325;&#2371;&#2340;&#2367;&#2325; &#2352;&#2375;&#2337;&#2367;&#2351;&#2379;&#2343;&#2352;&#2381;&#2350;&#2367;&#2340;&#2366; &#2325;&#2368; &#2326;&#2379;&#2332;</span>"
        }
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "C",
      "solution": "<p>Michael Faraday's experiment in 1831 demonstrated electromagnetic induction, showing that a changing magnetic field induces an electric current in a conductor. This principle is the foundation of electric generators and transformers.</p>",
      "images": [],
      "subject": null,
      "topic": null,
      "difficulty": null
    },
    {
